171   3.3   Ownership
172
173   For question 𝑞 and direction 𝑑, 𝑊𝑞,𝑑 is the paired mean change in 𝑃(yes) of 𝑞 under a write along 𝑑,
174   where 𝑃(yes) = 𝜎(ℓyes − ℓno ) at the first answer position and each log-probability is the maximum
175   over case and leading-space variants that tokenise to one token. The 6 × 6 matrix 𝑊 over concept
176   questions and concept directions is the object of interest (Figure 4). Ownership is the diagonal margin
177
178
                                             𝑂 𝑞 = 𝑊𝑞,𝑞 − max 𝑊𝑞,𝑑 ,                                      (4)
                                                             𝑑≠𝑞
179
180   the advantage of the concept’s own direction over its strongest clinical competitor on the concept’s
181   own question. A large 𝑊𝑞,𝑞 shows that 𝑑 = 𝑞 can write the answer; only 𝑂 𝑞 > 0 shows that the label
182   names the effective direction. Uncertainty over patients is quantified by 2,000 bootstrap draws of test
183   rows with the maximum recomputed inside every draw, and the 30 contrasts 𝑊𝑞,𝑞 − 𝑊𝑞,𝑑 receive
184
      simultaneous 95% lower bounds from a centred studentised max-𝑇 bootstrap (Appendix E).
185
186   3.4   Graded outcomes
187
188   • Readable: the one-sided 95% lower bound of 𝑆 is above zero, with at least ten positives and ten
189     negatives among the test rows.
190   • Answerable: the one-sided 95% lower bound of the clean-answer AUROC on the yes/no question
191     is above 0.5, under the same support requirement.
192   • Owned: the write meets the steering reference—𝑊𝑞,𝑞 > 0, above the 95th percentile of the 119
193     random writes, and above the absolute sham—and every simultaneous lower bound of 𝑊𝑞,𝑞 − 𝑊𝑞,𝑑
194     is positive. A cell with any simultaneous upper bound below zero has a stronger competitor; the
195     rest are unresolved.
196
197   The grades are defined for one direction construction, one relative dose per module with a five-point
198   sweep, six templates, pooled consumed-token features, and one primary and one connector locus;
199
      Section 5.6 scores four alternative direction constructions by the same rules. Every block additionally
      reports the signed dose range of 𝑂 𝑞 over 𝛼 ∈ {−0.5, . . . , +0.5}, the standard deviation of 𝑂 𝑞 over
200
      the three fit seeds (seeds 1 and 2 resample the training units with replacement and refit the scaler and
201
      the six probes), ownership and the absolute write effects at the connector locus, the label gap (under
202   the concept’s own write, the mean shift of the answer margin ℓyes − ℓno on label-positive rows minus
203   that on label-negative rows, in logits, median over concepts), and the wording (is versus show) and
204   mapping (A-present versus B-present) contrasts of 𝑂 𝑞 across six question templates.
205
206
      3.5   Acceptance gates
207
208
      Seven checks run on 16 held-out preflight rows at both loci before block scoring (Table 3): bitwise
209
      determinism; identity at 𝛼 = 0; reach; batched-versus-single agreement of the answer logits; image-
210   free semantic mapping of every template; throughput; and fp32 recomputation of the answer logits.
211   Reach requires the write to change the consumer’s input and answer logits without changing anything
212   outside the consumed tokens. We declare batched-versus-single deviations above 0.25 and neutralise
213   them by scoring every condition of a question in one fixed batch composition. We mark templates that
214   fail semantic mapping INELIGIBLE and close them as such. We grade a checkpoint whose yes/no
215   template fails on its eligible B-positive template (IB for Llama 3.2 Vision). Its scorecard records the
      template used. The block’s scorecard records every declared deviation.


