      4     Models, Datasets, and Scale
217
218
      Checkpoints. We evaluate 21 instruction-tuned checkpoints from nine families (Table 4; full list in
219
      Table 2): Qwen2.5-VL 3B/7B/32B/72B, Qwen3-VL 4B/8B/32B, InternVL3.5 8B/14B/38B, Gemma 3
220
      4B/12B/27B, MedGemma 4B/27B, Lingshu 7B/32B, LLaVA-1.5 7B/13B, LLaVA-Med 1.5 7B, and
221   Llama 3.2 Vision 11B. Three families are medical fine-tunes (MedGemma, Lingshu, LLaVA-Med);
222   the rest are general-purpose. The families span five vision-tower designs and three consumer designs
223   (token insertion after a merger, token insertion after a projector, and cross-attention). Consumed-token
224   counts range from 144 to 4,096 per image. Each checkpoint runs in bf16 at a pinned revision with its
225   native processor settings and a fixed image resolution per family.
226
227   Datasets and concepts. NIH ChestX-ray14 (Wang et al., 2017) and CheXpert Plus provide chest
228   radiographs with report-derived labels. We evaluate six findings on each (NIH: Effusion, Atelectasis,
229   Pneumothorax, Cardiomegaly, Mass, Nodule; CheXpert: Effusion, Atelectasis, Pneumothorax,
230   Cardiomegaly, Consolidation, Edema). COCO provides the natural-image control, with binary
231   presence labels for six object categories (person, dog, car, chair, bottle, bicycle). Seeded hashes of
232   patient (or image) identity select one fixed cohort per dataset: 20,000 training rows (18,212 on NIH,
233   every training patient after the split), 400 calibration rows, 600 test rows, and 16 preflight rows. Every
234   checkpoint shares these patient-disjoint splits. The chest sets use one frontal study per patient.
235
236   Questions and templates. We ask a yes/no question about each concept using six templates that
237   cross two wordings (is there versus does the image show) with three answer interfaces (yes/no,
238   A-present, B-present). The primary grade uses the is/yes-no template. The other five provide
239   the wording and mapping contrasts. The main write dose is 𝛼 = +0.25. The dose module adds
240
      𝛼 ∈ {−0.5, −0.25, −0.1, +0.1, +0.5}.
241
242   Scale. A block pairs one checkpoint with one dataset. Each block scores the 6 × 6 write matrix,
243   the sham, and 119 random directions on 600 test rows (457,200 outcomes). NIH and COCO blocks
244   also run the calibration, dose, refit, connector-locus, and template modules; CheXpert blocks add
245   calibration. The campaign comprises 61 blocks, 366 scored concept cells, about 30 million scored
246
      answers, and roughly 3,000 A100-hours. Twenty checkpoints are complete on all three datasets.
      Llama 3.2 Vision 11B is graded on its eligible B-positive template. We run Qwen2.5-VL-72B on NIH
247
      only. The largest checkpoint run is 72B (Appendix A; label handling, registration, and the released
248
      artifact are in Appendix A.1).
249
250
251   5     Results
252
253   5.1   Reading is not writing
254
255   Table 1 reports scores for every checkpoint on every dataset. Figure 2 shows every concept cell across
256   the three grades. Both chest datasets show the same pattern. Clinical concepts are readable in 138
257   of 246 cells (56%): probe selectivity exceeds matched random-label controls by 0.1–0.3 for most
258   findings in most families. The models also answer: 153 of the 246 cells with a scored write matrix
259   (62%) have a clean-answer AUROC whose lower bound exceeds chance. Of these answerable cells,
260   101 are also readable. Yet only 29 cells (12%) are owned. In 163 cells (66%), the verdict is stronger
261
      competitor—a direction fitted for another finding moves the concept’s answer more than its own
      direction does. Readability and answerability do not suffice for writing: only 21 of the 101 readable
262
      and answerable cells are owned (21%), compared with 8 of the remaining 145 (6%). Ownership
263
      is the rarest and least stable of the three grades on the chest sets. An effect floor of 0.05 on both
264   𝑊𝑞,𝑞 and 𝑂 𝑞 leaves 18 of the 29 owned chest cells (7% of 246), compared with 78 of the 101 owned
265   COCO cells (65%). Under both probe refits on resampled patients, 3 of the 10 owned NIH cells retain
266   ownership, compared with 87 of 101 on COCO (Table 14). Clinical writes are not selective on the
267   chest sets. Of a clinical write’s answer change across the six questions, 24% lands on its own question
268   on NIH and 22% on CheXpert, compared with 78% on COCO (Figure 7). We call this pattern the
269   attribution deficit. The model exposes the concept to a probe. The probe’s direction is not a handle
      on the concept. Written back, it moves other findings’ answers as much as its own.


