478
      across sizes within each family; dots mark owned cells and hatching marks ceiling cells (clean answer
479
      beyond 0.99 or 0.01 on more than 90% of rows).
480
481   are more saturated than chest blocks. Restricting each cell to unsaturated rows preserves all 29 owned
482   chest cells. Refits change 𝑂 𝑞 by a median of 0.03. Column selectivity is the share of a direction’s
483   absolute effect that falls on its own question. It is 0.10 on NIH and 0.12 on CheXpert, versus 0.64 on
484   COCO. On CheXpert, rows with a known label preserve the sign of 𝑂 𝑞 in 114 of 120 cells. Radiologist
485   labels on 200 CheXpert films reproduce ownership in 112 of 114 cells (Table 25). Rescoring in fp32
      and at batch size one changes 𝑊 by at most 0.056 and alters 0 of 96 grades (Table 23).


                                                                                              9
      Under review as a conference paper at ICLR 2027




486
      Alternative direction constructions. The deficit is not specific to the logistic normal. Four
487   alternative directions use the same projected, train-scaled features and write protocol (Table 16): the
488   difference of class means, the Haufe pattern Σ𝜷 𝑐 (Haufe et al., 2014), the normal orthogonalised
489   against the other five normals, and the normal refitted on features residualised against the other five
490   labels. Across the 60 scored blocks (20 per dataset), each construction owns 6–16% of chest cells
491   and 44–85% of COCO cells. The difference-of-means and pattern directions have cosines of 0.1–0.4
492   with the logistic normal. Each owns more NIH cells and fewer COCO cells than the logistic normal.
493   Lifted as displacements, these directions respectively own 40 and 34 of 228 chest cells and 58 and 49
494   of 114 COCO cells (Table 17). Every construction preserves the chest-to-COCO gap. At least one
495
      construction owns 25 of 120 cells on NIH and 41 of 120 on CheXpert, compared with 108 of 120 on
      COCO. Extending the clinical family to every additional dataset label with enough support retains
496
      5 of the 6 owned cells in the 8 scored blocks (Table 21). Projection, sex, and age join the findings
497
      in one nine-direction family. The attributes own 13 of 90 cells; findings own 16 of 180. On NIH,
498   ownership is 8/48 for attributes and 6/96 for findings (Table 20). We weight writes along the logistic
499   directions by the softmax of token probe scores or restrict writes to the top quarter of tokens at four
500   times the weight (twice the Frobenius norm of the uniform write). These writes own 0 of 6 chest
501   concepts and 6 of 6 COCO objects in Qwen2.5-VL-7B and Qwen3-VL-8B (Table 22). Concentrating
502   the write where the probe reads does not create a clinical handle.
503
504   The direction the reader responds to. The same representation contains the reader’s handle. For
505   each question, we fit the model’s own clean answer margin using ridge regression on the same features
506   (3,000 training rows). We lift the fitted direction like a probe normal and write it at the same dose
507   against the same reference family (Table 18). This answer direction 𝑎 𝑞 is a handle where the label
508   direction is not. Across the 36 chest cells in the 9 scored blocks (Table 26), 𝑎 𝑞 is owned in 19 and the
509   label direction in 9. Its write beats the strongest logistic competitor in 30. Across the five held-out
510   templates, 𝑎 𝑞 remains owned in 76 of 95 chest and 89 of 90 COCO (cell, template) pairs (Table 19).
511   In Qwen2.5-VL-7B, it is owned for 3 of six NIH concepts (Effusion 𝑂 𝑎 = +0.29 with an own write of
512
      0.81) and all 6 CheXpert concepts. In Lingshu 32B, it is owned for 3 NIH and 5 CheXpert concepts.
      In Gemma 3 12B, it is owned for 2 NIH and 0 CheXpert concepts. On the chest sets, its raw cosine
513
      with the probe normal is 0.06–0.07 in Qwen2.5-VL-7B and 0.30–0.33 in Lingshu 32B, compared
514
      with 0.54–0.64 on COCO. Whitening by the training covariance gives 0.36–0.43 on the chest sets
515   and 0.81 on COCO. The answer direction reads the label less accurately than the probe. It reads
516   the model’s own answer more accurately (Table 24). On natural images, the readable and writable
517   directions coincide. On chest radiographs, the model answers along a direction the probe does not
518   read.
519
520
521   6   Implications
522
523   For steering, model building, and clinical evaluation. A probe direction that reads a clinical
524   concept is not a handle on it. Probe accuracy and answer accuracy, the two quantities most often
525   reported for medical VLMs, do not establish whether a handle exists. Both are high in this grid,
526   yet ownership is 12%. Beating random and sham controls shows that a steering write reaches the
527
      answer, not that the label names the direction. At the same locus and dose, for the same patients, a
      direction fitted for another finding moves the same answer as much or more in two thirds of clinical
528
      cells. The matched-competitor test is cheap once competitors’ directions exist. Studies using probe
529
      normals as interventions should report the write matrix 𝑊, compare ownership 𝑂 𝑞 across layers,
530   doses, and models, and pair every clinical write with a natural-image control. The same write vector
531   produces different ownership in different language models. A vision tower that exposes a finding
532   linearly therefore does not make its direction a handle.
533
534
535   7   Conclusion
536
537   Reading a clinical concept from a vision-language model’s visual representation does not make its
538   probe direction a handle. Across 21 checkpoints and two chest-radiograph datasets, concepts are
539   readable in 56% and answerable in 62% of cells but owned in 12%. The same procedure owns 84%
      of natural-object cells. The direction the model answers along is a handle and is nearly orthogonal to


                                                        10
      Under review as a conference paper at ICLR 2027




540
      the probe direction on chest radiographs. Checkpoints sharing a vision tower still differ in what they
541   own, so the reader of the representation decides ownership.
542
543
544   Reproducibility statement
545
546   The registered protocol file fixes the loci, projection, probe settings, dose grid, reference family,
547   templates, modules, gates, and decision rules; its identifier and code commit are recorded in every
548
      block’s scorecard. The release contains the evaluation code and adapters, the protocol file, the cohort
      manifests with their seeds, the fitted directions, the per-image outcomes of every module, every block’s
549
      scorecard and coverage file, the rendered prompts, and the pinned checkpoint revisions (Table 2);
550
      every table and figure in the paper is regenerated from those files by the scripts in the release.
551
552
553   Ethics statement
554
555   The study uses three public datasets under their published terms: NIH ChestX-ray14 (NIH Clinical
556   Center), CheXpert Plus (Stanford AIMI, accessed through its data-use agreement), and COCO
557
      (annotations CC BY 4.0). All radiographs are de-identified by their providers; no protected health
      information was accessed, and no image or report is redistributed. The write test evaluates model
558
      behaviour under activation edits and is not a diagnostic tool; its grades describe what a probe direction
559
      does inside a model and carry no claim of clinical benefit.
560
