1115
       A.9     Per-image examples
1116
1117   Figure 8 shows grades on individual test images. For a chest example, the pool comprises test rows
1118   where the finding is labelled present, the probe reads it (probability above 0.5), and the clean answer
1119   is No. For a natural-image example, the pool comprises rows where the probe reads the label correctly
1120   and the clean answer is No. Rows in each pool are split by whether the concept’s own write or the
1121   strongest competing write raises the answer more. The row shown is nearest the median gap among
1122   rows where either write raises the answer by at least 0.2. Each example’s last line reports how many
1123   pool rows share its ordering, giving each example its own base rate.
1124
1125   B       Seed study: registered protocol
1126
1127   The two-model study that motivated the evaluation registered the procedures below; Appendix C
1128   reports its results.
1129
1130   Data and split. We use NIH ChestX-ray14 (Wang et al., 2017), whose findings are mined from
1131   radiology reports. A deterministic hash of patient identity assigns rows to disjoint train, validation,
1132   and test partitions. The registered manifest contains 18,212 training images and 5,343 test images from
1133   1,659 test patients. The three cells pair LLaVA-1.5-7B with Effusion and Edema, and Qwen2.5-VL-7B
       with Effusion. All reported uncertainty clusters by patient. Prompts appear in Appendix D.


