1330   where each log probability is the maximum over case and leading-space variants that tokenize to one
1331   token; the endpoint is its paired mean change from the common unsteered baseline. Original cells use
1332   concept doses {−1, −0.5, −0.25, −0.1, 0, 0.1, 0.25, 0.5, 1} on 200 held-out test images. Controls are
1333   evaluated at the six nonzero doses excluding ±0.1: 20 isotropic random unit directions, one sham
1334   formed by a fixed coordinate permutation of ŵ𝑐 and renormalization, and fixed alternative clinical
1335   probe normals. Every control receives the same signed dose on the same rows. Dose monotonicity
1336   over [−0.5, 0.5] is descriptive.
1337
1338   Original-cell decision rule. Let Δ𝑐 (𝛼) be the target direction’s paired mean probability change,
1339
       and let A = {−1, −0.5, −0.25, 0.25, 0.5, 1} be the six controlled doses. The primary effect is
       max 𝛼∈ A sign(𝛼)Δ𝑐 (𝛼), attained at dose 𝛼★. A cell clears the generic intervention gate when this
1340
       selected effect exceeds the 95th percentile of the 20 random directions’ mean probability changes
1341
       at that same 𝛼★, each multiplied by sign(𝛼★), and the maximum absolute sham effect over A. The
1342   random directions are not separately maximized over doses. This asymmetric selection defines a
1343   registered reference gate, not a cross-dose-corrected 5% test. The conjunction is fixed across the
1344   three cells. The independent supplement below instead evaluates a dose locked before observing its
1345   patients.
1346
1347   Direction-specificity supplement. The initial experiment prespecified the random and sham
1348   comparisons; its clinical-direction analysis was descriptive. The Nodule response motivated a
1349   separately registered supplement at the locked Qwen dose, 𝛼 = +0.25. We select one row by
       label-blind hash for each of 400 ChestX-ray14 test patients absent from the original 200-image


