1548
       Answer-encoding crossover. We next register a paired sensitivity study on 200 patients from the
       causal-ownership matrix cohort; the other 200 form a patient-disjoint capability-calibration set. This
1549
       is not the earlier Effusion specificity cohort. Six questions cross the original yes/no prompt with
1550
       A-present and B-present prompts, which exchange the finding-to-letter mapping. Each uses both
1551   𝛼 = ±0.25, six clinical directions, 20 random directions, and a sham. A question enters the primary
1552   endpoint only if both clean coded mappings have AUROC one-sided 95% lower bounds above 0.5
1553   and at least ten patients per class on calibration. At 𝛼 = +0.25, we compare squared mean effects
1554   in components that retain or reverse raw A-minus-B sign under the mapping exchange, using an
1555   unbiased estimator and 2,000 joint patient-bootstrap draws. Appendix C defines the components
1556   and full control rule. Descriptive clinical competition uses the contrast in Equation 8, replacing
1557   probability change with finding-present logit-margin change within each prompt.
1558
1559
       Independent Mass confirmation. We prospectively test Mass on 200 patients absent from all
1560   preceding Qwen intervention cohorts, retaining the crossover’s 200 calibration patients. Two question
1561   wordings (show, is) cross explicit yes/no, A-present, and B-present instructions; the original yes/no
1562   prompt is a seventh anchor. At 𝛼 = +0.25, each cell receives six clinical directions, 119 random
1563   directions, and a sham. The four coded cells share 20 clinical comparisons with simultaneous 95%
1564   lower bounds. Confirmation requires positive bounds and Mass effects above every random effect and
1565   absolute sham, either across all four coded cells or both original-wording cells. Appendix C specifies
       calibration and paired descriptive contrasts.


