1728
       D     Prompts, Scores, and Notation
1729
1730
       D.1   A common question and answer endpoint
1731
1732   At the first answer position, let ℓyes and ℓno denote the maximum log probabilities among the valid single-
1733   token case and leading-space variants of each answer. The registered score is 𝑃(yes) = 𝜎(ℓyes − ℓno ),
1734   where 𝜎 is the logistic sigmoid. The original Edema cell uses “pulmonary edema,” and the closure
1735   experiment uses “consolidation” in the same template. Taking the best valid token variant for each
1736   answer makes the endpoint insensitive to which listed capitalization or leading-space form receives
1737   the highest score. This endpoint measures the relative support for yes versus no at the first answer
1738   position; it does not measure the accuracy of a generated clinical report.
1739   The shared interface makes the question–direction grid interpretable: changing a clinical direction
1740   does not change the question being scored. Differences across rows reflect different clinical questions
1741   (questions are rows of 𝑊, directions are columns), so the ownership test compares directions within
1742   each row before drawing a conclusion about the named concept.
1743
1744   D.2   Reading the intervention notation
1745
1746   Table 31 collects the notation introduced in the article. The distinction between question 𝑞 and
1747   direction 𝑑 is central: a large 𝑊𝑞,𝑑 shows that a direction moves an answer, while 𝑂 𝑞 asks whether
1748
       the direction bearing that answer’s clinical name moves it more than its competitors. A positive
       ownership margin alone is not the full confirmation rule; the uncertainty and non-semantic control
1749
       comparisons must also pass. Keeping these roles separate allows the following analyses to distinguish
1750
       readability, intervention strength, and semantic attribution.
1751
1752
1753                                  Table 31: Notation used in the manuscript.
1754    Symbol     Definition
1755    𝑞, 𝑑, 𝑐    Clinical question, steering direction, and probe concept, respectively.
1756    h𝑖𝑡        Consumed visual representation for image 𝑖 and visual token 𝑡.
1757    𝑅, 𝒔       Fixed Gaussian projection and train-fitted featurewise scale.
1758
        ŵ𝑐        Unit probe-normal direction for concept 𝑐 after lifting to the consumed representation.
        𝛼          Signed intervention dose; |𝛼| is the perturbation norm relative to each token norm.
1759    𝑊𝑞,𝑑       Paired mean change in question 𝑞’s 𝑃(yes) under direction 𝑑.
1760    𝑂𝑞         Ownership margin 𝑊𝑞,𝑞 − max𝑑≠𝑞 𝑊𝑞,𝑑 ; positive values favor the named direction.
1761    𝑆          Real-label AUROC minus the mean AUROC of the 20 nuisance-type control tasks.
1762    𝛾𝑖         Positive-minus-negative paired representation displacement along the Consolidation normal.
1763
1764
1765   The notation separates three levels of evidence: 𝑆 describes label availability, 𝑊𝑞,𝑑 describes an
1766   intervention effect, and 𝑂 𝑞 compares the named direction with clinical alternatives. The remaining
1767
       appendix follows this progression while keeping the evaluation cohorts explicit.
